            <div xml:id="d557">

                <pb facs="0685" n="565" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg565"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">815.00 Revolutions/29: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lay</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-04-24T20:00:00-06:00">April 24, 1931—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 25—10:45 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>72. No combat engagements have been reported in the last 24 hours. Military
                    headquarters here estimates the present total number of rebels in the entire
                    country as 1,000. The bulk of these are to the west of San Pedro Sula, retiring
                    west and southwest apparently followed but not pursued by the Government forces
                    in several columns.</p>
                <p>An American citizen, John Edward Wright, time-keeper on the Lerida farm of the
                    United Fruit Company was murdered this morning by an unknown assailant. Lerida
                    is about 50 miles up the Truxillo Railroad from Puerto Castilla. The Legation’s
                    information does not indicate whether this murder was an act of the rebels or
                    was merely another of the fairly frequent crimes normally occurring in this
                    lawless region.</p>
                <p>The fruit companies have resumed normal operations in all the banana districts
                    except in one section to the south of San Pedro Sula.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lay</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
